/**
 * The Leighton-Micali Hash Based Signature (LMS) requires
 * a LM-OTS signature (Leighton-Micali One Time Signature).
 *
 * Reference:
 * [LMS] RFC 8554: Leighton-Micali Hash-Based Signatures
 *       McGrew, Curcio, Fluhrer.
 *       April 1, 2019.
 *       @see https://doi.org/10.17487/RFC8554
 *
 * [NIST] NIST SP800-208: Recommendation for Stateful Hash Based Signature Schemes.
 *        Cooper et. al.
 *        October 2020
 *        @see https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.SP.800-208
 */
interface module Primitive::Asymmetric::Signature::LM_OTS::Parameters where

/*
 * The signature system uses the parameters `n` and `w`, which are both
 * positive integers.  The algorithm description also makes use of the
 * internal parameters `p` and `ls`, which are dependent on `n` and `w`.
 *
 * Ref: [LMS] Section 4.1
 */

/**
 * The number of bytes of the output of the hash function.
 */
type n: #

type constraint (fin n, n > 0)

/**
 * Here, we actually paramterize on a type _w, that maps
 * onto the "real" parameter w. This is done to ensure that
 * the types `w` actually fall into the specified set.
 */
type _w: #

type constraint (_w < 4)

/**
 * This is the number of independent Winternitz chains used in the
 * signature; it will be the number of w-bit digits needed to hold
 * the n-bit hash (u in the below equations), along with the
 * number of digits needed to hold the checksum (v in the below
 * equations)
 */
type p: # // Stubbed out

type constraint (fin p, p > 0)

/**
 * This is the size of the shift needed to move the checksum so
 * that it appears in the checksum digits
 */
type ls: # // Stubbed out

type constraint (fin ls)


/**
 * The width (in bits) of the Winternitz coefficients; that is,
 * the number of bits from the hash or checksum that is used with a
 * single Winternitz chain.  It is a member of the set
 *  { 1, 2, 4, 8 }.
 *
 * Here we observe that this set is the first four powers of two.
 */
type w = 2 ^^ _w

/**
 * This constraint is added to appease Cryptol's typechecker.
 */
type constraint (8 % w == 0)

/**
 *
 * Per Section 3.2 of [LMS]:
 *
 * A typecode is an unsigned integer that is associated with a
 * particular data format.  The format of the LM-OTS, LMS, and HSS
 * signatures and public keys all begin with a typecode that indicates
 * the precise details used in that format.  These typecodes are
 * represented as four-byte unsigned integers in network byte order.
 *
 * The typecode is referenced throughout the RFC to specify details
 * such as the choice of hash function, data format, etc...rather than
 * tabulate these, to support more flexible instances, we take the typecode
 * as a parameter, and expect the implementor to set the other parameters
 * in accord with the standard if they desire standards conformance.
 */
typecode: [32]
